SAK ERP – Local Installation & Deployment Checklists

Document Purpose
This document provides two separate checklists:
1) Provider Checklist (our team) – tasks we must perform for installation and deployment.
2) Client Checklist (their team) – prerequisites and preparations required from client side.

====================================================================
PORTION 1: PROVIDER CHECKLIST (OUR TEAM)
====================================================================

A. Project Readiness
[ ] Freeze release version/branch to deploy.
[ ] Verify latest production-tested build is stable.
[ ] Confirm environment variable matrix for API and Web is complete.
[ ] Confirm migration scripts order and rollback notes are ready.
[ ] Prepare client-specific configuration values (domain/IP, tenant setup, branding if any).

B. Infrastructure Validation (Before Touching Client Server)
[ ] Validate target OS compatibility (Windows Server 2019/2022 confirmed).
[ ] Confirm minimum sizing: 4 vCPU, 8 GB RAM, 80+ GB SSD.
[ ] Confirm network model (internet-facing vs LAN/VPN only).
[ ] Confirm required ports and firewall approach.
[ ] Confirm backup path and restore strategy before first migration.

C. Installation Tasks (Execution)
[ ] Install runtime stack: Node.js 20.x, pnpm 9.x, PM2.
[ ] Install system utilities: git, curl, 7zip/tar, build tools.
[ ] Install and configure reverse proxy (IIS + URL Rewrite + ARR, or Nginx for Windows).
[ ] Configure API and Web process definitions in PM2.
[ ] Configure startup on reboot and persistence (PM2 startup task/service on Windows).

D. Application Deployment
[ ] Pull/upload application artifacts/source.
[ ] Install dependencies with lockfile (`pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`).
[ ] Build API and Web.
[ ] Apply DB migrations/SQL in approved sequence.
[ ] Start/restart services and verify process health.

E. Security Hardening
[ ] Enforce HTTPS and redirect HTTP to HTTPS.
[ ] Add secure headers at reverse proxy level.
[ ] Close direct access to internal app ports (3000/4000) from public network.
[ ] Create least-privilege OS user for app runtime.
[ ] Store secrets in protected env files / secret store.

F. Functional Validation (Post-Deploy)
[ ] Login and role/permission validation.
[ ] Core flows validation: PR, PO/GRN, Smart Job Order, QC, UID traceability.
[ ] Email/notification path validation.
[ ] File upload/download validation.
[ ] Confirm no blocking errors in application logs.

G. Handover & Documentation
[ ] Provide runbook with start/stop/restart commands.
[ ] Provide environment variable reference (masked values).
[ ] Provide backup and restore SOP.
[ ] Provide update/patch process and rollback process.
[ ] Obtain sign-off from client technical owner.

H. Hypercare (After Go-Live)
[ ] Monitor logs and performance for agreed stabilization period.
[ ] Resolve priority incidents within SLA.
[ ] Deliver final deployment report with open issues (if any).


====================================================================
PORTION 2: CLIENT CHECKLIST (THEIR TEAM)
====================================================================

A. Server & Network Preparation
[ ] Provision server (Windows Server 2019/2022).
[ ] Ensure minimum resources: 4 vCPU, 8 GB RAM, 80+ GB SSD.
[ ] Share static IP/hostname details.
[ ] Open required ports: 3389 (RDP), 80, 443 (and internal routing plan for app ports).
[ ] Confirm DNS readiness (if domain-based access required).

B. Access & Credentials
[ ] Provide RDP + local administrator access to designated technical contacts.
[ ] Ensure installer account has local admin rights for installation window.
[ ] Share security policy constraints (password rotation, SSH policy, endpoint restrictions).
[ ] Share approved maintenance window and rollback decision owner.

C. Data & Integration Inputs
[ ] Confirm database hosting model (managed vs self-hosted).
[ ] Provide DB credentials/connection details (if client-managed DB).
[ ] Provide email configuration (SMTP/OAuth sender account) if client-managed.
[ ] Provide storage details (S3/Supabase/local policy) if client-managed.
[ ] Provide required master data templates (items, vendors, users, etc.) if part of onboarding.

D. Security & Compliance Inputs
[ ] Provide SSL certificate preference (IIS certificate / organization certificate / Let’s Encrypt if applicable).
[ ] Provide IP allowlist requirements (if any).
[ ] Provide audit/log retention policy.
[ ] Provide backup retention policy and RPO/RTO targets.
[ ] Confirm compliance requirements (if any) affecting deployment.

E. Operational Readiness
[ ] Nominate application owner and IT owner.
[ ] Nominate key business UAT users.
[ ] Prepare user list and role mapping for initial access.
[ ] Confirm escalation matrix for go-live support.
[ ] Confirm who approves production cutover.

F. Go-Live Readiness
[ ] Complete UAT sign-off.
[ ] Validate business process checklist with provider team.
[ ] Confirm final cutover date/time.
[ ] Confirm communication to end users.
[ ] Keep rollback window reserved in case of issues.

G. Post-Go-Live Commitments
[ ] Ensure server monitoring ownership (CPU, RAM, disk, uptime).
[ ] Ensure backup jobs are running and tested.
[ ] Ensure periodic Windows Update/security patching process exists.
[ ] Ensure incident reporting channel to provider is active.


====================================================================
Quick Acceptance Criteria
====================================================================
Deployment is considered complete when:
[ ] Application is reachable on approved URL/IP.
[ ] Admin and standard users can login and perform assigned flows.
[ ] No blocker errors in logs for core workflows.
[ ] Backup and restart procedures are verified.
[ ] Both provider and client sign-off is captured.


